# Caddy config for the production box. Fetched from main by user_data at first
# boot (alongside docker-compose.prod.yml) and mounted read-only into the caddy
# container at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.
#
# Caddy does automatic HTTPS: naming a site here is enough for it to obtain a
# Let's Encrypt certificate over ACME, serve TLS, and renew unattended. The
# proof-of-control is the HTTP-01 challenge — LE requests a token back over
# plain HTTP on :80, which the security group already allows. That's why no DNS
# plugin and no DNS-provider API token are needed on the box.

# LIVE on the Let's Encrypt PRODUCTION CA (Caddy's default — no acme_ca directive).
# Certificates are browser-trusted; renewal is automatic at ~1/3 of remaining life.
#
# ⚠️ RATE LIMIT — production allows only **5 duplicate certificates per week per
# name**. Caddy keeps its certs in the caddy_data volume on the root EBS, so every
# instance replacement destroys them and forces a fresh issuance — and
# `user_data_replace_on_change` replaces the box on ANY user_data edit. Replacing
# the instance a handful of times in one week would therefore exhaust the quota and
# lock HTTPS issuance out until the window rolls.
#
# So: batch user_data changes, and tune THIS file in place on the box
# (/opt/task-tracker/Caddyfile + `docker compose up -d --force-recreate caddy`),
# which reloads Caddy without touching the instance. If replacements ever need to be
# frequent, either persist /data on a separate EBS volume that survives replacement,
# or temporarily re-add the staging CA while iterating:
#     acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory

# sslip.io is a wildcard DNS service: any name of the form <ip>.sslip.io resolves
# to that IP, with no registration and no record to maintain. So this name already
# points at the Elastic IP — the hostname IS the address.
#
# WHY NOT DuckDNS (the original choice, 2026-07-14): Let's Encrypt could not issue
# for mbtasktracker.duckdns.org — its validators kept timing out on the mandatory
# CAA lookup. Measured against DuckDNS's own authoritative nameservers: 6 of 9 were
# entirely unresponsive and the rest dropped ~half their queries (5 answers out of
# 27). Recursive resolvers hide this by caching and retrying across all nine, which
# is why `dig` and browsers looked fine; LE does fresh uncached lookups from several
# vantage points under a tight timeout and simply gives up. sslip.io's nameservers
# answered 9 of 9 on the same test.
#
# TRADE-OFF: the IP is baked into the hostname, so this URL only survives as long as
# the Elastic IP does. That is already a standing rule (never `terraform destroy` —
# it releases the EIP), but it means moving to a real domain later is the fix if the
# name ever needs to outlive the address.
100.31.18.48.sslip.io {
	# `app` resolves over the compose network; the app no longer publishes a host
	# port, so this is the only path to it. Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For and
	# X-Forwarded-Proto on the way through — which is exactly the one proxy hop
	# TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1 tells Express to trust.
	reverse_proxy app:3000
}
